# Applied Ethics: Dilemmas of Life, Technology, and Society

> Principles are cheap at the seminar table and expensive in the
> clinic, the codebase, and the boardroom. Applied ethics works
> through real dilemmas — end-of-life care, scarce organs, biased
> algorithms, a warming planet — where every option costs something.

## 1. The Method: Principles in Tension

Applied ethics does not start from scratch. It brings **principles**
to a case, lets them collide, and argues for a verdict — the method
Beauchamp and Childress call *principlism*.

| Principle | Demand | Tension with |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Autonomy | Respect informed, voluntary choice | Paternalism "for your own good" |
| Beneficence | Act for the person's good | Autonomy — refusing beneficial care |
| Non-maleficence | First, do no harm | Every intervention carries some harm |
| Justice | Distribute benefits and burdens fairly | Beneficence — the many vs the one |

[definition]
**Informed consent** is the operational test of autonomy: the person
understands the options, grasps the consequences, and chooses free
of coercion. Consent without understanding — or under pressure —
fails the test even if the form is signed.
[/definition]

A good verdict names the principles on each side, explains which
weight it gives and why, and stays consistent with the next similar
case.

## 2. The Trolley Problem — and Why It Is Not an Ethics Kit

The classic case: a trolley will kill five; diverting it to a side
track kills one. Pull the switch? Most say yes. In the **footbridge**
variant, stopping the trolley requires pushing a large man off a
bridge; most say no — though the arithmetic is identical.

| Reading | Verdict |
| --- | --- |
| Utilitarian | Pull, and push — one death beats five, always |
| Doctrine of double effect | Pull (harm foreseen as a side effect), not push (harm used as a means) |
| Virtue ethics | The question is what a wise, compassionate person would do here |

[warning]
The trolley problem trains the *shape* of moral reasoning — means vs
side effects, doing vs allowing — but it is a stress test, not a
policy tool. Real dilemmas involve uncertainty, institutions, and
responsibilities that the stylised case quietly deletes. An
autonomous-vehicle "trolley setting" is marketing, not ethics.
[/warning]

## 3. Bioethics: Dilemmas at the End of Life

**Euthanasia** — literally a good death — divides first by consent
and then by means.

| Type | Who chooses | Means | Legal status (varies by jurisdiction) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Voluntary, active | Patient requests | Lethal injection | Legal in a handful of countries |
| Passive | Withholding futile treatment | Withdrawal of life support | Widely permitted |
| Non-voluntary | Others decide | — | Deeply contested (e.g. infants, coma) |

The standard arguments: autonomy favours the right to decide the
manner of one's death; beneficence favours ending intractable
suffering; the deontological objection is that killing treats a
person as disposable; the slippery-slope objection fears pressure on
the vulnerable. The **doctrine of double effect** explains why
withdrawing support and administering lethal drugs are treated so
differently — one foresees death as a side effect, the other intends
it as the means.

## 4. Bioethics: Allocating Scarce Organs

One donor liver, two patients — a child with a rare disease and a
60-year-old with two dependents. Scarcity forces justice into the
open: every allocation rule *is* an ethical theory in disguise.

| Rule | What it maximises | What it sacrifices |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First come, first served | Procedural fairness | Outcomes |
| Sickest first | Rescue of the dying | May spend the organ on low-benefit cases |
| Best prognosis / life-years | Total benefit | Discriminates against the old and the sick |
| Lottery | Equal chances | Ignores need entirely |

Real systems blend the criteria — urgency, expected benefit, waiting
time — and must resist the quiet corruptions of fame, wealth, and
connections. The pandemic made this everyone's problem: ventilator
triage protocols were organ allocation writ large.

## 5. AI Ethics: Bias and Fairness

A model trained on the past inherits the past's injustices — and
then launders them as mathematics. Recidivism scores that flag
Black defendants at higher rates, hiring screens trained on
male-dominated CVs, face recognition with higher error rates on
darker skin: the mechanism is the same — **biased data in, biased
decision out**.

@chart donut title="Share of reported AI incidents by sector (illustrative)" labels:
Content and platforms
Policing and justice
Hiring and work
Health
Finance
Other

data:
30
16
14
13
12
15
@/chart

| Fairness notion | Definition | Why it conflicts with the others |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Group parity | Equal approval rates across groups | Collides with differing base rates |
| Equalised odds | Equal error rates across groups | Provable impossibility when base rates differ (Kleinberg et al.) |
| Individual fairness | Similar people treated similarly | Needs a contested metric of "similar" |

[important]
"Fix the data" is necessary but insufficient: historical injustice is
not a data-quality bug. Fairness choices are *political* — which
groups count, which error matters more — and hiding them inside a
model closes a decision that a society should keep open.
[/important]

## 6. AI Ethics: Privacy and Accountability

Training data is scraped from lives; inference re-identifies the
anonymised; a model can be wrong about you with no one to appeal
to. Two questions define the field: *who may know what?* and *who
answers when the system errs?*

| Concern | Harm | Candidate safeguard |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mass collection | Surveillance chilling free life | Data minimisation, purpose limitation |
| Inference | Prediction of health, belief, orientation | Right to explanation, opt-outs |
| Opaque decisions | No appeal, no learning | Auditability, human review |
| Diffuse responsibility | "The model decided" | Named owners and accountability chains |

The **accountability gap**: harm was caused, but the developer points
to the deployer, the deployer to the data, the data to society.
Ethics insists the gap be filled by design — logging, audits,
liability rules — not by luck.

## 7. Environmental Ethics: Anthropocentrism to Ecocentrism

Who counts, morally? Each answer redraws our duties to nature.

| Position | What has value | Verdict on a forest |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Anthropocentrism | Humans only | Preserve the watershed — for us |
| Sentientism | All feeling beings | Animal suffering in logging matters |
| Biocentrism | Every living thing | Each tree has a good of its own |
| Ecocentrism | Whole systems and species — Leopold's "land ethic" | The forest as a community has standing |

@diagram tree id="moral-standing" layout="tree" aria="Expanding circles of moral standing from humans to the biosphere"
  "Moral standing" -> "Anthropocentrism"
  "Moral standing" -> "Sentientism"
  "Moral standing" -> "Biocentrism"
  "Moral standing" -> "Ecocentrism"
@/diagram

[example]
Climate change is the stress test. A purely anthropocentric case —
protect economies and coastlines — already demands deep cuts; a
sentientist case adds future humans and animals; an ecocentric case
adds the collapse of whole ecosystems. Notice how the *conclusion*
rarely moves while the *ground* deepens: much of environmental ethics
is an argument about justification, not action.
[/example]

## 8. Business Ethics: Whistle-blowing and Responsibility

A safety engineer discovers the brake tests were falsified. Report
up and be ignored? Resign quietly? Leak to the regulator? Business
ethics asks when loyalty to an institution must yield to the public.

| Option | What it protects | What it risks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Internal reporting | The institution, careers first | Time — and complicity if ignored |
| External whistle-blowing | The public | Retaliation — hence legal protections |
| Resignation | Personal integrity | Neither the public nor the firm learns |

[tip]
Most ethicists set a threshold: go public when the harm is serious,
internal channels are exhausted or complicit, and the evidence is
documented and yours to share. Below that line, patience and
persistence; above it, silence is complicity.
[/tip]

[summary]
Applied ethics is principled triage: autonomy, beneficence,
non-maleficence, and justice collide in clinics, algorithms,
ecosystems, and firms — and the discipline is to show which
principle yields, to whom, and why. The trolley trains the
reasoning; the real cases — euthanasia, organs, biased models, a
warming planet — supply the stakes.
[/summary]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Four principles | Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice |
| Trolley lesson | Harm as means vs side effect — a trainer, not a policy |
| Euthanasia | Consent and means; double effect separates them |
| Organ allocation | Every rule is a justice theory in disguise |
| AI fairness | Notions conflict; the choice is political, not mathematical |
| Ecocentrism | Value beyond humans — the land ethic |
